 \u2014 a rushing-yards spot.</p><p>Efficiency edge to LA \u2014 a 49% success-rate offense against a NYG defense that allows 46% \u2014 points should come.</p><p><strong>Jaxson Dart</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 LA allow <strong>239 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><p><strong>Malik Nabers</strong> draws a plus matchup \u2014 LA concede <strong>68 yds/game to WR1s</strong> (league ~58), and NYG funnel 29% of targets his way. A rec-yards spot.</p><p><strong>Theo Johnson</strong> has room to work \u2014 LA allow <strong>41 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Tyrone Tracy Jr.</strong> eats as a receiver too \u2014 LA give up 23 rec yds/game to backs, and NYG target the RB on 18% of throws.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Kyren Williams</strong> is a bell-cow (68% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.53 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Malik Nabers</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 LA surrender 0.59 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>Fade the NYG run \u2014 as 10-point dogs projected to chase, <strong>Tyrone Tracy Jr.</strong>'s carries get squeezed, though his receiving work ticks up in catch-up mode.</p><p>A middling 48 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><p><strong>Indoors</strong> \u2014 a dome removes weather variance and historically nudges passing efficiency up; a small tailwind for the passing games.</p><h3>LA \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>LA are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (44% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Kyren Williams</strong> (14 carries, 63 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though LA's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Puka Nacua</strong> projects for <strong>12 targets and 119 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Colby Parkinson</strong> (TE, 6 targets, 45 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>NYG \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>NYG are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (42% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 NYG project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>Tyrone Tracy Jr.</strong>'s rushing projection (13 carries, 57 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Malik Nabers</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, NYG funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>10 targets for 75 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Theo Johnson</strong> (TE, 7 targets, 52 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 10-poi